        {
          "id": "the_fuel_sulfur_sulfate_fractions_and_the_split_that_does_not_conserve_mass",
          "description": "`SulfatePMCalculator`'s headline arithmetic, and the one genuinely new computation in this rung. The four `sulfatefractions` columns are pinned at a pre-2004 gasoline cohort, a 2004+ gasoline cohort and a 2007+ diesel cohort, then the sulfur adjustment they drive, then the two fractions the split uses. THE SULFATE TAKES THE ADJUSTED FRACTION AND THE RESIDUE THE UNADJUSTED ONE: on MY 1980 gasoline the sulfate share is 0.0272484 and the residue share is 0.92, which sum to 0.9472 and not to 1. Deleting the adjustment moves a cell by a factor of 2.385 and using the adjusted fraction in both places by 62 % (docs/process-pm-exhaust.md 7.2, rows 18 and 19). The water arm is written and dead -- `H2OnonECPMFraction` is 0 on all six rows -- and the `greatest(..., 0)` clamp never binds. Electricity has no row at all: `rt_hasSulfateFractions` is 0, the fractions are 0, and the divisor is guarded to 1 so that 0/0 never reaches the product.",
